       To avoid blindly closing file descriptors in the range of possible file
       descriptors,  this  is sometimes implemented (on Linux) by listing open
       file descriptors in _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_f_d_/ and calling cclloossee(2)  on  each  one.
       cclloossee__rraannggee()  can take care of this without requiring _/_p_r_o_c and within
       a single system call, which provides significant performance benefits.

   CClloossiinngg ffiillee ddeessccrriippttoorrss bbeeffoorree eexxeecc
       File descriptors can be closed safely using

           /* we don’t want anything past stderr here */
           close_range(3, ~0U, CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE);
           execve(....);

       CCLLOOSSEE__RRAANNGGEE__UUNNSSHHAARREE is conceptually equivalent to

           unshare(CLONE_FILES);
           close_range(first, last, 0);

       but can be more efficient: if the unshared range extends past the  cur‐
       rent  maximum number of file descriptors allocated in the caller’s file
       descriptor table (the common case when _l_a_s_t is ~0U),  the  kernel  will
       unshare a new file descriptor table for the caller up to _f_i_r_s_t, copying
       as  few  file descriptors as possible.  This avoids subsequent cclloossee(2)
       calls entirely; the whole operation is complete once the table  is  un‐
       shared.

   CClloossiinngg ffiilleess oonn _ee_xx_ee_cc
       This  is  particularly  useful  in  cases where multiple pre‐eexxeecc setup
       steps risk conflicting with each other.  For example, setting up a sseecc‐‐
       ccoommpp(2) profile can conflict with a cclloossee__rraannggee() call: if the file de‐
       scriptors are closed before the sseeccccoommpp(2) profile is set up, the  pro‐
       file setup can’t use them itself, or control their closure; if the file
       descriptors  are closed afterwards, the seccomp profile can’t block the
       cclloossee__rraannggee() call or any fallbacks.  Using CCLLOOSSEE__RRAANNGGEE__CCLLOOEEXXEECC  avoids
       this:  the  descriptors  can be marked before the sseeccccoommpp(2) profile is
       set up, and the profile can control access to cclloossee__rraannggee() without af‐
       fecting the calling process.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The program shown below opens the files named in its command‐line argu‐
       ments, displays the list of files that  it  has  opened  (by  iterating
       through  the  entries in _/_p_r_o_c_/_P_I_D_/_f_d), uses cclloossee__rraannggee() to close all
       file descriptors greater than or equal to 3, and then  once  more  dis‐
       plays  the  process’s list of open files.  The following example demon‐
       strates the use of the program:

           $ ttoouucchh //ttmmpp//aa //ttmmpp//bb //ttmmpp//cc;
           $ ..//aa..oouutt //ttmmpp//aa //ttmmpp//bb //ttmmpp//cc;
           /tmp/a opened as FD 3
           /tmp/b opened as FD 4
           /tmp/c opened as FD 5
           /proc/self/fd/0 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/1 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/2 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/3 ==> /tmp/a
           /proc/self/fd/4 ==> /tmp/b
           /proc/self/fd/5 ==> /tmp/c
           /proc/self/fd/6 ==> /proc/9005/fd
           ========= About to call close_range() =======
           /proc/self/fd/0 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/1 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/2 ==> /dev/pts/1
           /proc/self/fd/3 ==> /proc/9005/fd

       Note that the lines showing the pathname _/_p_r_o_c_/_9_0_0_5_/_f_d result from  the
       calls to ooppeennddiirr(3).

